<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="PDE-solutions-complete">
    <title>Solution Methods to PDEs (Complete)</title>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Like ODEs, solving PDEs is a complicated task. However, there is one method that helps in many 
                        cases, and is very relevant for the PDEs encountered in quantum mechanics.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise>
            <title>Separation of Variables</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        A common theme for solving PDEs is to do something to reduce it to multiple ODEs. This is 
                        what the method of separation of variables does, which we will illustrate with several examples.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task workspace="10in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>u_t = ku_{xx}</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            We start by assuming a solution form of <m>u(x,t) = \varphi(x) \cdot \tau(t)</m>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            With this assumption, we re-write the PDE as 
                            <md>
                               \varphi(x)\tau'(t) = k\varphi''\tau(t)
                            </md>.
                            
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Now we can separate time and space variables to get the equation
                            <md>
                                \frac{1}{k} \frac{\tau'(t)}{\tau(t)} =\frac{\varphi''(x)}{\varphi(x)}
                            </md>.
                            Since <m>x</m> and <m>t</m> are distinct variables, the only way these expressions can equal is 
                            if they are both a constant. That is, 
                            <md>
                                \frac{1}{k} \frac{\tau'(t)}{\tau(t)} =\frac{\varphi''(x)}{\varphi(x)} = -\lambda
                            </md>.
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Now we have to ODEs:
                            <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The time ODE <m>\tau'(t) = -k\lambda \tau(t)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The space ODE <m>\varphi''(x) + \lambda \varphi(x) =0</m>.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            If we can solve each of the ODE's above, then we'll have a general product solution for the PDE 
                            of the form <m>u(x,t) = \varphi(x)\tau(t)</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="10in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>u_{tt} = c^2u_{xx}</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Assuming a product solution, this PDE becomes
                            <md>
                                \varphi(x)\tau''(t) = c^2 \varphi''(x) \tau(t)
                            </md>.
                            Separating variable and setting equal to <m>-\lambda</m> gives the two ODEs
                            <md>
                                <mrow>  \tau''(t) + c^2 \lambda \tau(t)\amp= 0</mrow>
                                <mrow>\varphi''(x) + \lambda \varphi(x) \amp=0 </mrow>
                            </md>.
                            
                            
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="10in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>i \overline{h}\psi_t = \frac{-\overline{h}}{2m}\psi_{xx}</m>
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Call <m>k_1=i \overline{h}</m> and <m>k_2=\frac{-\overline{h}}{2m}</m>. Then assuming a product solution
                            we can re-write the PDE as
                            <md>
                                k_1\varphi(x)\tau'(t) = k_2 \varphi''(x) \tau(t)
                            </md>.
                            Separating variables and setting equal to <m>-\lambda</m> give the two ODEs
                            <md>
                                <mrow>  \tau'(t) \amp= \frac{-\lambda}{k_1}\tau(t)</mrow>
                                <mrow>\varphi''(x) + \frac{\lambda}{k_2} \varphi(x) \amp=0 </mrow>
                            </md>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                
            </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="10in">
        <title>Using Boundary/Initial Conditions</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Consider the PDE <m>u_t =ku_{xx}</m> with
                <md>
                    <mrow>u(0,t) \amp= u(L,t) = 0 </mrow>
                    <mrow>u(x,0) \amp=2\sin\left(\frac{3\pi}{L} x\right) </mrow>
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                We saw that the separation of variables method for this PDE gave us two ODEs:
                <ol>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The time ODE <m>\tau'(t) = -\lambda \tau(t)</m>.
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        This is separable, with solution <m>\tau(t) = ce^{-\lambda t}</m>. But for what 
                                        values of <m>\lambda</m> is this a valid solution? We need the next ODE to find out.
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                                <li>
                                    <p>
                                        The space ODE <m>\varphi''(x) + \lambda \varphi(x) =0</m>.
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        Now that we have boundary conditions for the PDE, we can use them to get boundary 
                                        conditions for this ODE:
                                        <md>
                                            <mrow>u(0,t) =0 \amp\rightarrow \varphi(0)\tau(t) =0 \amp\rightarrow \varphi(0) = 0</mrow>
                                            <mrow>u(L,t) =0 \amp\rightarrow \varphi(L)\tau(t) =0 \amp\rightarrow \varphi(L) = 0</mrow>
                                        </md>
                                        
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        The characteristic equation for the second-order linear space ODE is <m>r^2 + \lambda =0</m>,
                                        which means the characteristic roots are <m>r = \pm\sqrt{-\lambda}</m>. The solution form depends on 
                                        whether <m>\lambda</m> is negative, zero, or positive.
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        If <m>\lambda \lt 0</m> or <m>\lambda =0</m>, the boundary conditions imply only a trivial solution 
                                        for the space equation (<m>\varphi(x) =0</m>; see in-class notes for details). 
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        If <m>\lambda \gt 0</m>, then the characteristic roots are <m>r=\pm \sqrt{\lambda}i</m>. For notational
                                        convenience, define <m>\mu = \sqrt{\lambda}</m>, so <m>r=\pm \mu i</m>. Then the general solution is
                                        <md>
                                            \varphi(x) = A\cos(\mu x) + B \sin(\mu x)
                                        </md>.
                                        The boundary condition <m>\varphi(0)=0</m> forces <m>A=0</m>, and <m>\varphi(L)=0</m> forces
                                        <m>B\sin(\mu L)=0</m>. This can be true for <m>B\neq 0</m>! In particular, <m>\sin(\mu L) =0</m> 
                                        when <m>\mu L = n \pi</m>, for integers <m>n \geq 1</m>.
                                        
                                    </p>
                                    <p>
                                        So we get infinitely many solutions (eigenfunctions)
                                        <md>
                                            \varphi_n(x)= \sin\left(\frac{n \pi}{L} x\right) 
                                        </md>
                                        with associated eigenvalues <m>\lambda_n = \mu_n^2 = \left(\frac{n\pi}{L}\right)^2</m>. So our product
                                        solutions to the PDE are
                                        <md>
                                            u_n(x,t) = c_n \tau(t) \cdot \varphi(x) = c_n e^{-\left(\frac{n \pi}{L}\right)^2t} \sin\left(\frac{n \pi}{L} x\right)
                                        </md>,
                                         
                                         which satisfy the heat equation PDE and the boundary conditions. To get a specific 
                                         solution, we must also satisfy the initial condition <m>u(x,0) =2\sin\left(\frac{3\pi}{L} x\right)</m>.
                                         But notice our general solutions <m>u_n(x,0) = c_n\sin\left(\frac{n \pi}{L} x\right)</m>, so we can satisfy all
                                         conditions by picking our particular solution to be <m>u_3(x,t) = 2e^{-\left(\frac{3 \pi}{L}\right)^2t} \sin\left(\frac{3 \pi}{L} x\right)</m>.
                                         See <url href="https://www.desmos.com/3d/q30na0jias">Desmos</url> for a visual of our solution.
                                        
                                        
                                    </p>
                                </li>
                            </ol>
                We were somewhat lucky that the initial condition was easy to satisfy by picking just one of our 
                product solutions. What if the initial condition <m>u(x,0)</m> is not so convenient? This is where Fourier series
                become useful, which we'll see the basics of next time.
                 </p>
        </solution>
        
    </exercise>




       
    </worksheet>
